WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.547
Talking to the CEO and Executives.

2
00:00:02.827 --> 00:00:06.667
Day 70 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:08.417 --> 00:00:09.441
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:15.375
Executives have 30 seconds of attention for your topic and 100 other problems waiting.

5
00:00:15.655 --> 00:00:20.817
Lead with the answer, translate tech into business, and make the decision easy for them.

6
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:23.021
Part 1.

7
00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:24.943
What executives care about.

8
00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:26.876
Money:

9
00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:28.564
Revenue, cost, ROI.

10
00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:31.617
This saves 12 lakh rupees a year in cloud spend.

11
00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:32.793
Customers:

12
00:00:33.073 --> 00:00:35.164
Experience, churn, NPS.

13
00:00:35.444 --> 00:00:37.620
Checkout failures dropped 60%.

14
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.604
Risk:

15
00:00:38.884 --> 00:00:41.999
Security, compliance, outages, reputation.

16
00:00:42.279 --> 00:00:43.132
Speed:

17
00:00:43.412 --> 00:00:45.417
Time-to-market, competitive edge.

18
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:47.642
Part 2.

19
00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:49.394
The executive formula.

20
00:00:50.644 --> 00:00:51.561
Step 1:

21
00:00:51.841 --> 00:00:54.679
Headline: the answer or the ask in one sentence.

22
00:00:54.959 --> 00:00:55.876
Step 2:

23
00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:58.865
Impact: money, customers, risk, in numbers.

24
00:00:59.145 --> 00:01:00.063
Step 3:

25
00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:01.217
Options:

26
00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:03.588
2 or 3, with your recommendation.

27
00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:04.828
Step 4:

28
00:01:05.108 --> 00:01:07.881
Ask: the exact decision you need, and by when.

29
00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:10.127
Part 3.

30
00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:11.986
Translate tech into business.

31
00:01:13.236 --> 00:01:14.601
Here is a quick table:

32
00:01:14.881 --> 00:01:17.868
Engineer says, and CEO hears, and Say instead.

33
00:01:18.148 --> 00:01:19.919
We need to refactor the monolith.

34
00:01:20.199 --> 00:01:21.265
CEO hears:

35
00:01:21.545 --> 00:01:23.380
Engineers want to play with code.

36
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.620
Say instead:

37
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.524
Every new feature takes 3 weeks instead of 1.

38
00:01:27.804 --> 00:01:30.257
Splitting the system doubles our release speed.

39
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:31.689
We have tech debt.

40
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:33.036
CEO hears:

41
00:01:33.316 --> 00:01:34.425
Vague complaint.

42
00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:35.665
Say instead:

43
00:01:35.945 --> 00:01:45.631
Fixing 3 fragile areas cuts outages by about half, and each outage costs us ~4 lakh rupees. p99 latency is 3 seconds.

44
00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:47.020
CEO hears: ?

45
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.260
Say instead:

46
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:54.385
1 in 100 customers waits 3 seconds at checkout, and 20% of those abandon.

47
00:01:54.665 --> 00:01:55.988
We need Kubernetes.

48
00:01:56.268 --> 00:01:57.335
CEO hears:

49
00:01:57.615 --> 00:01:58.980
Expensive buzzword.

50
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.220
Say instead:

51
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.727
We can handle sale-day traffic without manual work, and stop over-paying for idle servers.

52
00:02:06.007 --> 00:02:08.289
The DB is a single point of failure.

53
00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:09.679
CEO hears: ?

54
00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:10.919
Say instead:

55
00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:14.356
If one server dies, the whole site is down for hours.

56
00:02:14.636 --> 00:02:17.196
A backup costs 30 thousand rupees per month.

57
00:02:18.546 --> 00:02:19.463
Part 4.

58
00:02:19.743 --> 00:02:20.533
Scripts.

59
00:02:21.783 --> 00:02:23.809
30-second elevator update.

60
00:02:24.089 --> 00:02:25.028
You could say:

61
00:02:25.308 --> 00:02:27.996
The new delivery-tracking feature launches Monday.

62
00:02:28.276 --> 00:02:32.244
Early tests show 25% fewer 'where is my order' calls.

63
00:02:32.524 --> 00:02:37.793
One risk: map API costs may rise 15%, and we have a fallback ready.

64
00:02:38.073 --> 00:02:39.716
No decision needed today.

65
00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:42.325
Explaining an outage.

66
00:02:42.605 --> 00:02:43.543
You could say:

67
00:02:43.823 --> 00:02:49.178
Yesterday, checkout was down for 40 minutes, from 7:10 to 7:50 pm.

68
00:02:49.458 --> 00:02:54.215
About 1,200 orders failed, roughly 9 lakh rupees in sales.

69
00:02:54.495 --> 00:02:57.482
Cause: a database update ran during peak hours.

70
00:02:57.762 --> 00:03:03.714
We've recovered, and 70% of affected customers have already re-ordered after our coupon email.

71
00:03:03.994 --> 00:03:10.138
To prevent repeats, all DB changes now go through an automated check and run only off-peak.

72
00:03:10.418 --> 00:03:13.319
I'd like approval for 2 sprints of reliability work.

73
00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:14.858
Can I count on that?

74
00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:18.192
Asking for budget or headcount.

75
00:03:18.472 --> 00:03:19.411
You could say:

76
00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:22.741
I'm asking for 2 more backend engineers for Q1.

77
00:03:23.021 --> 00:03:29.357
Right now the team spends 40% of its time on support tickets, so the loyalty program slips to Q3.

78
00:03:29.637 --> 00:03:37.253
With 2 hires, it ships in Q2, which product estimates adds 1.5 crore rupees in annual repeat revenue.

79
00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:39.880
Cost is about 60 lakh rupees a year.

80
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.381
Can we approve this by the 15th so we can start hiring?

81
00:03:44.731 --> 00:03:47.249
Answering \Why is this late?\.

82
00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:48.467
You could say:

83
00:03:48.747 --> 00:03:53.227
It's 2 weeks late because the payment partner changed their API mid-project.

84
00:03:53.507 --> 00:03:58.094
We've finished the rework, and the new date is the 22nd, with high confidence.

85
00:03:58.374 --> 00:04:01.574
Going forward, we're adding partner-change checks to our planning.

86
00:04:02.924 --> 00:04:04.673
No blame, no long story.

87
00:04:04.953 --> 00:04:07.449
Cause, new date, and the fix for next time.

88
00:04:08.799 --> 00:04:10.143
When you don't know the answer.

89
00:04:10.423 --> 00:04:11.362
You could say:

90
00:04:11.642 --> 00:04:13.391
I don't have that number right now.

91
00:04:13.671 --> 00:04:15.719
I'll get it to you by 3 pm today.

92
00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:19.608
Never guess numbers in front of executives.

93
00:04:20.958 --> 00:04:21.918
Part 5.

94
00:04:22.198 --> 00:04:23.947
Before you walk in: checklist.

95
00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:27.523
Can I say my main point in one sentence?

96
00:04:27.803 --> 00:04:30.853
Do I have numbers (₹, %, users, hours)?

97
00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:33.992
Do I have 2 to 3 options and a recommendation?

98
00:04:34.272 --> 00:04:36.768
Do I know the exact decision I'm asking for?

99
00:04:37.048 --> 00:04:40.376
Have I pre-wired it with my manager and other key people?

100
00:04:40.656 --> 00:04:43.429
Is there a 1-page summary I can leave behind?

101
00:04:43.709 --> 00:04:47.336
Have I prepared answers for What if we do nothing? and What's the risk?

102
00:04:48.686 --> 00:04:49.625
Part 6.

103
00:04:49.905 --> 00:04:51.206
Mistakes to avoid.

104
00:04:52.456 --> 00:04:53.309
Avoid:

105
00:04:53.589 --> 00:04:58.965
Starting with background (So, in 2023 we chose MongoDB because...).

106
00:04:59.245 --> 00:05:00.099
Avoid:

107
00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:02.107
Jargon without translation.

108
00:05:02.387 --> 00:05:03.240
Avoid:

109
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.589
Showing 30 slides when 3 will do.

110
00:05:05.869 --> 00:05:06.723
Avoid:

111
00:05:07.003 --> 00:05:09.883
Surprising your manager by going over their head.

112
00:05:10.163 --> 00:05:11.016
Avoid:

113
00:05:11.296 --> 00:05:13.259
Getting defensive when interrupted.

114
00:05:13.539 --> 00:05:14.947
Interruptions are normal.

115
00:05:15.227 --> 00:05:16.443
Answer and move on.

116
00:05:17.793 --> 00:05:18.731
Part 7.

117
00:05:19.011 --> 00:05:20.590
For experienced engineers.

118
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.405
Learn the company's top 3 goals this year and tie every proposal to one of them.

119
00:05:26.685 --> 00:05:27.581
Say less.

120
00:05:27.861 --> 00:05:31.381
Yes, it's on track is a complete answer if that's what was asked.

121
00:05:31.661 --> 00:05:37.656
Build relationships before you need them: a coffee chat with a VP is worth more than a slide deck.

122
00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:41.136
Bring the so what for the business, not just the technical what.

123
00:05:42.486 --> 00:05:43.361
Part 8.

124
00:05:43.641 --> 00:05:45.027
Practice conversation:

125
00:05:45.307 --> 00:05:47.611
The 30-second update in a corridor.

126
00:05:48.861 --> 00:05:50.888
Here is how the conversation goes.

127
00:05:51.168 --> 00:05:52.363
Ashish, right?

128
00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:54.840
How's the delivery tracking project?

129
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.699
It launches on Monday.

130
00:05:56.979 --> 00:06:01.331
In testing, where is my order calls dropped by 25%.

131
00:06:01.611 --> 00:06:02.528
Excellent.

132
00:06:02.808 --> 00:06:03.832
Any risks?

133
00:06:04.112 --> 00:06:07.184
One: map costs may go up about 15%.

134
00:06:07.464 --> 00:06:10.216
We already have a cheaper backup provider ready.

135
00:06:11.566 --> 00:06:13.166
The conversation continues.

136
00:06:13.446 --> 00:06:14.193
Good.

137
00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:15.987
Anything you need from me?

138
00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:18.059
No decision needed today.

139
00:06:18.339 --> 00:06:21.091
I'll share the launch results next Friday.

140
00:06:21.371 --> 00:06:22.417
Great work.

141
00:06:22.697 --> 00:06:23.977
Keep me posted.

142
00:06:25.327 --> 00:06:26.863
That's the end of this lesson.

143
00:06:27.143 --> 00:06:31.815
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

144
00:06:32.095 --> 00:06:38.196
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

